# Machine-specific i3 settings.
#
# The main config pulls this in with:
#     include_if_exists ~/.config/i3.local.conf
# so this file is OPTIONAL: with it absent, i3 starts with no per-machine
# setup at all (which is exactly right for the WSL/VNC session, whose single
# virtual screen has no named outputs to configure).
#
# To use it:
#     cp ~/.config/i3/i3.local.conf.example ~/.config/i3.local.conf
#     $EDITOR ~/.config/i3.local.conf
#
# It is a real i3 config, not a shell script: `set`, `bindsym`, `exec` and
# `workspace ... output` all work normally. Anything here wins over the main
# config only for directives that appear later -- i3 applies these in file
# order, and the include sits near the top, so prefer re-stating a directive
# over expecting an override.
#
# It is intentionally NOT in the repo: its contents are per-machine.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-monitor layout
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Output names come from `xrandr --query`. DP-* is almost always the laptop or
# a DisplayPort panel, DVI-D-* a DVI port, HDMI-* an HDMI TV or monitor.
#
# exec --no-startup-id xrandr --output DP-1 --primary --mode 1920x1080 --pos 0x0 --auto
# exec --no-startup-id xrandr --output DVI-D-0 --right-of DP-1 --mode 1920x1080 --auto
#
# Keep workspaces 1 and 2 on their own screens, so $mod+Prior / $mod+Next
# (workspace next_on_output) stay useful and $mod+m can move a workspace to
# the monitor next to it.
#
# workspace 1 output DP-1
# workspace 2 output DVI-D-0

# Mode switch and resolution cycling, against your own helper scripts.
#
# bindsym $mod+Shift+s exec --no-startup-id ~/.dotfiles/bin/monitor
# bindsym $mod+Shift+n exec --no-startup-id ~/.dotfiles/bin/resolution

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compositor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# picom is what draws window transparency, and i3 draws none of its own. It is
# started unconditionally by the main config, which is right on a real GPU
# X session.
#
# On the WSL/VNC session it is not: that display is a software-rasterised
# Xvnc, so picom composites every frame on the CPU for no visible gain (this
# config sets no window opacity, so there is nothing for it to blend). To
# switch it off there, uncomment:
#
# # exec --no-startup-id pkill -x picom
